      "worked": "Both equations give $\\binom{n}{4}=\\binom{n}{6}$, with $n\\ge6$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{n!}{4!(n-4)!}&=\\frac{n!}{6!(n-6)!}\\\\(n-4)(n-5)&=30\\\\n^2-9n-10&=0\\\\(n-10)(n+1)&=0\\\\n&=10\\quad\\text{since }n\\ge6.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $\\binom{10}{k}=\\binom{10}{4}=210$. Factorial form gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\binom{10}{10-k}&=\\frac{10!}{(10-k)!k!}=\\binom{10}{k}\\\\\\frac{\\binom{10}{k+1}}{\\binom{10}{k}}&=\\frac{10-k}{k+1}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe ratio is greater than $1$ for $k=0,1,2,3,4$, so the values increase strictly up to $k=5$ and then decrease symmetrically. Hence $210$ occurs only at $k=4$ and $k=6$.\n\nThe solutions are $(n,k)=(10,4)$ and $(10,6)$.",
